the spec was defective, and the arms said so BEFORE implementing. This is the first task
where fb-speccheck ran in the automated path. All four arms critiqued it; codex-luna named
both defects exactly:

  1. uncomputable -- clause 3 required Unknown.why to be derived from the Absent's stated
     reason, but Absent::NotAttempted is a unit variant with no reason.
  2. contradiction -- Unknown is documented as "the artefact could not be inspected at all"
     while the superset section maps NothingToMeasure and Untrusted onto it.

Both were ruled correct and recorded before dispatch. Neither was FIXED, because two arms
were still critiquing the live text and their implementation runs continue those sessions;
revising mid-stage would have left the field reading two documents. So this wave also
measures how a field handles a spec defect it has already named -- which is the more
interesting question anyway.

winner: glm-53-flash. Its `unknown_why` is one match that returns a specific honest string
for NotAttempted and FRAMES every other reason as "the artefact could not be inspected:
{stated}". That framing is the closest honest thing to defect 2: it makes the string
self-describing in a log line where the variant name is not visible, which is exactly what
the contradiction cost. gemini-38-flash triplicates three structurally identical
trim().is_empty() branches -- unreachable through measurement's own constructors, which
normalise via stated() -- and returns the bare reason with no framing. glm's critique found
that ("two sources for one rule that can drift apart"); gemini's critique of glm found
nothing and praised specifics.

